---
import ProjectArticle from "#layouts/project-article.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import book from "#assets/athens-portal/book.jpeg";
import discover from "#assets/athens-portal/discover.jpeg";
import homepage from "#assets/athens-portal/homepage.jpeg";
import logo from "#assets/athens-portal/logo-full.jpeg";
import reviews from "#assets/athens-portal/reviews.jpeg";
import Section from "#components/section.astro";
---

<ProjectArticle
  title="Athens Portal"
  description="A mobile app built for my bachelor's degree final paper."
  image={logo}
  imageAlt="Athens Portal logo"
>
  <Section name="Background">
    <p>
      As soon as I started college in 2020, the pandemic hit us hard. Remote
      classes were very rough at the start. Both professors and students that
      weren't "tech-aligned" struggled very hard to attend to classes. This
      improved over time but there was something that did not: finding reliable
      sources to our studies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Brazil is not a country where the majority of people have the habit of
      reading. This includes several factors as the pricing (books are usually
      expensive), lack of incentive from schools and (maybe) the fact that
      youngest people simply prefer to stay on their smartphones.
    </p>
    <p>
      In our case, professors often shared works that could help us on our
      studies, however most of those books were too expensive or were not
      available at the college's library. Communication sometimes was not easy,
      so we often forgot about those recommended books.
    </p>
    <p>
      The idea of creating an app to help us with this problem came from
      Guilherme Angeloni. He was one of few friends that I made in that class, a
      good and honest person, but he lacked experience in software development.
      We agreed that he would work on the research and I would develop the app.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section name="The app">
    <p>
      The premise of the app was creating a community capable of sharing books
      or other works in an environment built for it. People could find new works
      through "indexing", a system built to find them in other public sources on
      the web. At the time, we were only using one third-party API for this
      search.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once a book got succesfully indexed, it was them saved on our database and
      available to be reviewed. Any registered user could create a review about
      the work, giving a commentary and also assign a rating from 1 to 5 stars.
    </p>

    <p>
      A person could use this app without being registered, but it could not
      create reviews nor indexing new works.
    </p>
    <div class="gallery">
      <Image src={homepage} alt="An image of the homepage" />
      <Image src={discover} alt="An image of the Discover List screen" />
      <Image
        src={book}
        alt={`An image of the screen showing info about "The Hitchhiker's Guide to The Galaxy" `}
      />
      <Image
        src={reviews}
        alt={`An image of the "Recent reviews" section in the homepage`}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The homepage shows works divided by categories: highlights, most rated,
      recent reviews and recent indexes. The latter one just lists the latest
      works that were added to our database, while the other categories had a
      more complex algorithm under the hood.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another view is the Discovery List. Registered users could add in their
      profile what genres and types of work (books, magazines, letters etc.)
      they like. The server would generate a list of indexed works that are
      related to the user likings, based on their overall rating.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section name="Technical details">
    <p>The app was built using Dart/Flutter using Clean Architecture.</p>
    <p>We used a Node.js back-end built using Express</p>
    <!-- <p>For more info refer to this document</p> -->
  </Section>
</ProjectArticle>

<style is:global>
  .gallery {
    margin: 4rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    height: 300px;
    gap: 3rem;
    justify-content: center;

    img {
      width: auto;
      height: 300px;
    }
  }
</style>
